  "reasoning": "The patient presents with frequent sneezing, a sore throat, excessive mucus in the nose and throat, a swollen neck, and pain when swallowing. These symptoms strongly indicate an acute viral upper respiratory infection. While allergies can cause sneezing and a sore throat, they typically present with clear runny noses, itchy or watery eyes, and lack the thick mucus buildup and painful swollen lymph nodes characteristic of an infection. Furthermore, the patient explicitly describes having a 'bad cold,' and the symptom cluster aligns precisely with reference case [1], which maps identical symptoms (sneezing, nasal/throat mucus, sore throat, swollen neck) directly to a common cold. Therefore, common cold is the established diagnosis, and allergy is factually ruled out by the infectious nature of the symptoms.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]common cold[/DIAGNOSIS]"
